@MEDFORD MEDFORD file modeling an archaeologist and excavation
@MEDFORD-Version 1.0

@Contributor Sir Arthur John Evans
@Contributor-Role Principal excavator, Director
@Contributor-Association British School at Athens
@Contributor-Note Keeper of the Ashmolean Museum, Oxford, 1884-1908
@Contributor-Note Coined the term "Minoan" for the Bronze Age civilisation of Crete
@Contributor-Note Born 1851, Nash Mills, Hertfordshire, England; died 1941
@Contributor-Funding Self-financed through inherited family wealth (Dickinson paper mills)

@Contributor Duncan Mackenzie
@Contributor-Role Field supervisor; kept the excavation daybooks
@Contributor-Association British School at Athens

@Contributor Theodore Fyfe
@Contributor-Role Architect; recording of building and finds; early conservation shelter

@Contributor Manolis Akoumianakis
@Contributor-Role Local foreman
@Contributor-Note Known as "Manolaki"


@Site Knossos
@Site-Location Kephala hill, ~5 km (3 mi) south of Heraklion, Crete, Greece
@Site-Type Minoan palace complex ("Palace of Minos")
@Site-Period Neolithic through Late Bronze Age (occupied to ~1350 BCE)
@Site-Note First palace built ~1900 BCE; rebuilt after destruction ~1700 BCE
@Site-Note Earlier trial trenches dug by Minos Kalokairinos in 1878-1879


@Excavation Knossos main excavation
@Excavation-Director @Contributor{Sir Arthur John Evans}
@Excavation-StartDate 1900-03-23
@Excavation-StartDate-Note Most scholarly sources give 23 March 1900; some give 16 March 1900
@Excavation-EndDate 1905
@Excavation-EndDate-Note Core excavation took ~4-6 seasons; Evans continued restoration/study at the site until the 1930s
@Excavation-Method Open-area excavation with large local labour force
@Excavation-Permit Acquired 1900 after Crete's independence from Ottoman rule


@Find Throne Room
@Find-Date 1900-04
@Find-Note Room with a throne against the wall, uncovered within weeks of starting

@Find Linear B tablets
@Find-Type Inscribed clay tablets
@Find-Count ~3000 (across the campaign)
@Find-Note Script later named Linear B; Evans also defined Linear A and a pictographic script

@Find Frescoes
@Find-Type Wall paintings
@Find-Note Motifs include bull-leaping, double axes, women with snakes

@Find Material assemblage
@Find-Type Pottery, sealings, figurines, stone vessels, metal objects


@Paper The Palace of Minos at Knossos
@Paper-Author @Contributor{Sir Arthur John Evans}
@Paper-Type Excavation report (6 volumes)
@Paper-Note Primary publication of the Knossos excavations


@Data Sir Arthur Evans Archive
@Data-Repository Ashmolean Museum, Oxford
@Data-Note Includes excavation notebooks, photographs, and drawings
@Data-Note Mackenzie's daybooks are the principal day-to-day field record